The claim is supported because both the U.S. Surgeon General and the CDC did previously state that masks were not effective for the general public. On February 29, 2020, Surgeon General Jerome Adams tweeted that masks "are NOT effective in preventing general public from catching coronavirus," a statement documented by [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2020/04/02/coronavirus-us-surgeon-general-asks-cdc-to-see-if-face-masks-are-effective.html). Additionally, as reported by [Vox](https://www.vox.com/2020/4/3/21202792/coronavirus-masks-n95-trump-white-house-cdc-ppe-shortage), the CDC's early 2020 guidance did not recommend masks for healthy individuals, with a spokesperson explaining that surgical masks would not stop wearers from inhaling small airborne particles. While both the CDC and the Surgeon General shifted their official recommendations in April 2020 to advocate for mask-wearing due to growing evidence of asymptomatic transmission, as noted in [JAMA](https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2769440), the claim accurately represents their prior public health stances.